## Codex config quick reference
User config normally lives at $HOME/.codex/config.toml.
Profile files are $HOME/.codex/NAME.config.toml and are selected with codex --profile NAME.
Project config can live at .codex/config.toml, but project-scoped .codex layers are skipped when the project is untrusted.
Repeatable CLI overrides use codex -c key=value. Values are parsed as TOML when possible.
Common top-level keys include model, model_provider, model_reasoning_effort, approval_policy, sandbox_mode, profiles, projects, features, hooks, skills, mcp_servers, model_providers, tools, web_search, and project_doc_max_bytes.
Use --strict-config when checking whether this Codex version rejects unknown config keys.
Prefer --sandbox workspace-write plus --ask-for-approval on-request for normal interactive local work.
Use --ask-for-approval never for non-interactive automation only when the command scope is already safe.
Avoid --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox unless the user is inside an externally hardened sandbox or the existing wrapper intentionally uses it.

## Hooks quick reference
Hooks are configured under [features] hooks = true and [hooks] tables.
Supported lifecycle names to inspect: SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit, PreToolUse, PermissionRequest, PostToolUse, Stop, PreCompact, PostCompact, SubagentStart, SubagentStop.
Command hook groups use matcher plus nested hooks entries.
Command hook handler fields include type = "command", command, command_windows or commandWindows, timeout, statusMessage, and async.
Only command handlers run today. Prompt and agent handlers may parse but should not be treated as active execution.
Command hooks receive one JSON object on stdin. Usually relevant fields include session_id, transcript_path, cwd, hook_event_name, model, turn_id, permission_mode, tool_name, and tool_input depending on event.
PreToolUse and PostToolUse are useful for Bash, apply_patch/file-edit aliases, and MCP tool policy, but they are guardrails, not a complete enforcement boundary.
PermissionRequest hooks can allow or deny approval requests.
SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit, Stop, compaction, and subagent hooks can add additionalContext or systemMessage depending on event.
Plain stdout is ignored for many hook decisions; return valid JSON when influencing Codex behavior.

Minimal inline PreToolUse TOML example:
[features]
hooks = true

[[hooks.PreToolUse]]
matcher = "^Bash$"

[[hooks.PreToolUse.hooks]]
type = "command"
command = "/usr/bin/python3 .codex/hooks/pre_tool_use_policy.py"
timeout = 30
statusMessage = "Checking Bash command"

Minimal PostToolUse TOML example:
[[hooks.PostToolUse]]
matcher = "^Bash$"

[[hooks.PostToolUse.hooks]]
type = "command"
command = "/usr/bin/python3 .codex/hooks/post_tool_use_review.py"
timeout = 30
statusMessage = "Reviewing Bash output"

PermissionRequest allow JSON shape:
{ "hookSpecificOutput": { "hookEventName": "PermissionRequest", "decision": { "behavior": "allow" } } }

PermissionRequest deny JSON shape:
{ "hookSpecificOutput": { "hookEventName": "PermissionRequest", "decision": { "behavior": "deny", "message": "Blocked by repository policy." } } }

PostToolUse block JSON shape:
{ "decision": "block", "reason": "The Bash output needs review before continuing.", "hookSpecificOutput": { "hookEventName": "PostToolUse", "additionalContext": "The command updated generated files." } }

## Managed hooks and requirements quick reference
allow_managed_hooks_only belongs in requirements.toml, not normal config.toml.
Do not tell the user to put allow_managed_hooks_only in $HOME/.codex/config.toml.
Managed hooks use [hooks] managed_dir on macOS/Linux and windows_managed_dir on Windows.
Managed hook directories should be absolute and installed by enterprise tooling.
allow_managed_hooks_only skips user, project, session, and plugin hooks while still allowing managed hooks from requirements and managed config layers.
Conflicting managed hook directories should be treated as fail-closed config problems.
Do not edit enterprise requirements unless the user explicitly asks and you have inspected the exact layer.

## hooks.state safety
hooks.state is runtime/trust state, not the place to hand-author new hooks.
Duplicate [hooks.state] TOML keys can block Codex startup.
When startup fails with duplicate hooks.state keys, inspect the exact duplicate, back up the config, remove only the duplicate stale state entry, and preserve unrelated config.
Never blanket-delete all hook state unless the user explicitly asks after you explain the consequence.
Never append a second hooks.state entry with the same key.

## Skills quick reference
A skill is a folder containing SKILL.md.
SKILL.md should include YAML frontmatter with name and description, then concise instructions.
Repo skills are discovered under .agents/skills from the current working directory upward to the repo root.
User skills live under $HOME/.agents/skills.
Admin skills can live under /etc/codex/skills.
Bundled skills come with Codex.
Codex supports symlinked skill folders.
Disable a skill with [[skills.config]] path = "/path/to/skill/SKILL.md" and enabled = false.
Inspect [skills] include_instructions and [skills.bundled] enabled when debugging why skills are missing or too noisy.
Restart or start a new Codex session after config-only skill changes if the current session does not pick them up.

Minimal SKILL.md example:
---
name: codex-config-doctor
description: Use when diagnosing Codex config.toml, hooks, skills, AGENTS.md, or startup failures.
---
Inspect Codex version, safe config keys, project trust, hook tables, skill paths, and AGENTS.md layering before editing.

Minimal skill disable TOML:
[[skills.config]]
path = "/path/to/skill/SKILL.md"
enabled = false

## AGENTS.md quick reference
Global guidance can live at $HOME/.codex/AGENTS.md.
Temporary global override can live at $HOME/.codex/AGENTS.override.md.
Repository guidance usually lives at AGENTS.md.
Nested directories can use AGENTS.md or AGENTS.override.md for more specific guidance.
AGENTS.override.md overrides AGENTS.md at the same level.
project_doc_fallback_filenames can make additional filenames count as instruction files.
project_doc_max_bytes controls how much instruction text is loaded before truncation.
When AGENTS.md seems ignored, inspect current working directory, repo root, fallback filenames, overrides, trust, and byte limits.

## Workflow
1. Run the narrowest inspection command that proves the current Codex or repo state.
2. Classify the task: CLI usage, config, hooks, skills, AGENTS.md, SDK/app-server/runtime, install/link, or source edit.
3. Read before editing. For source edits, inspect the target file and git status first.
4. For config or hook work, inspect version, relevant config layers, project trust, and only the matching section of config.
5. For hook work, inspect hook config and hook scripts before changing either.
6. For skill work, inspect SKILL.md discovery paths and [skills] config before creating or disabling skills.
7. For AGENTS.md work, inspect global, repo, nested, override, fallback, and max-bytes settings.
8. Patch the smallest relevant file set.
9. Verify with the narrowest command first, then broader tests if source changed.
10. If command syntax fails, run the relevant --help and retry once.
11. If a missing imp command or link is involved, inspect package.json bin entries and $HOME/.bun/bin links before changing source.

